1. Disk Overflow Management (Log File Rotation & Deletion)
Scenario Analysis:
Log files grow continuously. If we simply delete files until usage drops below 80%, we might delete the most recent critical logs.
A better approach is log rotation (compress old logs) first, then delete the oldest logs if space is still insufficient. 
We must also avoid deleting the currently active log file.

Improved Pseudocode:
BEGIN
    SET threshold = 80%                      // Disk usage trigger
    SET min_free_space = 20%                 // Target free space

    // 1. Check current usage
    usage = GET_DISK_USAGE("/var/log")
    
    IF usage > threshold THEN
        // 2. Identify all log files (excluding the active one, e.g., "app.log")
        log_files = LIST_FILES("/var/log/*.log*") 
        SORT log_files BY last_modified_date ASCENDING  // Oldest first
        
        // 3. First pass: Compress uncompressed logs (.log to .log.gz)
        FOR each file IN log_files WHERE file DOES NOT end with ".gz" DO
            COMPRESS file TO file.gz
            DELETE original file
            usage = GET_DISK_USAGE("/var/log")
            IF usage <= threshold THEN
                BREAK
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR

        // 4. Second pass: If still over threshold, delete oldest compressed logs
        IF usage > threshold THEN
            log_files = LIST_FILES("/var/log/*.log.gz")
            SORT log_files BY last_modified_date ASCENDING  // Oldest first
            FOR each file IN log_files DO
                DELETE file                           // Delete oldest archive
                usage = GET_DISK_USAGE("/var/log")
                IF usage <= threshold THEN
                    BREAK
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
    ENDIF

    // 5. Alert admin if still over threshold after all deletions
    IF usage > threshold THEN
        SEND_ALERT("Manual cleanup required: disk usage at ", usage)
    ENDIF
END
Why this is better: It preserves recent data (via compression) and only deletes the oldest archived files as a last resort, 
2. Priority-Based CPU Scheduling (with Aging)
Scenario Analysis:
A naive priority scheduler can cause starvation (low-priority processes never run). To improve CPU efficiency and fairness, 
we must implement dynamic priority aging, where waiting processes gradually increase in priority. We also use a max-heap (priority queue) 
for O(log n) selection instead of sorting the whole list repeatedly.

Improved Pseudocode:
BEGIN
    // Input: List of processes with (PID, base_priority, remaining_time)
    INPUT process_list
    
    // Initialize priority queue (max-heap) based on effective priority
    priority_queue = EMPTY_HEAP()
    FOR each process IN process_list DO
        process.effective_priority = process.base_priority
        process.waiting_time = 0
        INSERT process INTO priority_queue
    ENDFOR

    WHILE priority_queue IS NOT EMPTY DO
        // 1. Select process with highest effective priority
        current = EXTRACT_MAX(priority_queue)
        
        // 2. Allocate CPU time (time quantum, e.g., 10ms)
        ALLOCATE CPU time_quantum TO current
        EXECUTE current FOR time_quantum
        current.remaining_time = current.remaining_time - time_quantum

        // 3. Aging: Increment waiting time for all other processes
        FOR each process IN priority_queue DO
            process.waiting_time = process.waiting_time + time_quantum
            // Boost priority by 1 for every 100ms waited (aging)
            process.effective_priority = process.base_priority 
                                         + (process.waiting_time / 100)
        ENDFOR

        // 4. Re-insert if not completed
        IF current.remaining_time > 0 THEN
            // Reset waiting time as it just ran
            current.waiting_time = 0 
            INSERT current INTO priority_queue
        ELSE
            PRINT "Process ", current.PID, " completed."
        ENDIF
    ENDWHILE
END

Q3. RAM Distribution among Virtual Machines
Scenario Analysis:
Total RAM is 16GB, with 4GB reserved for the host, leaving 12GB available. If we allocate a flat 2GB to every VM, we can only 
support 6 VMs (12/2 = 6). A better algorithm distributes the memory fairly based on demand, guaranteeing a minimum but allowing extra 
RAM to VMs that need it (weighted fair sharing).

Improved Pseudocode:

BEGIN
    // System constants
    total_RAM = 16 GB
    host_reserved = 4 GB
    available_RAM = total_RAM - host_reserved   // = 12 GB
    min_RAM_per_VM = 2 GB

    INPUT number_of_VMs
    
    // 1. Check if minimum requirements can be met
    IF number_of_VMs * min_RAM_per_VM > available_RAM THEN
        PRINT "Error: Insufficient memory for minimum requirements."
        PRINT "Maximum VMs supported: ", (available_RAM / min_RAM_per_VM)
        EXIT
    ENDIF

    // 2. Calculate leftover RAM after allocating minimums
    allocated_RAM = 0
    FOR i = 1 TO number_of_VMs DO
        VM[i].guaranteed = min_RAM_per_VM
        VM[i].allocated = min_RAM_per_VM
        allocated_RAM = allocated_RAM + min_RAM_per_VM
    ENDFOR

    extra_RAM = available_RAM - allocated_RAM   // RAM to distribute dynamically

    // 3. Distribute extra RAM based on VM priority/weight (e.g., equally or weighted)
    total_weight = SUM(VM[i].weight)            // Assume each VM has a weight
    FOR i = 1 TO number_of_VMs DO
        IF extra_RAM > 0 THEN
            share = (VM[i].weight / total_weight) * extra_RAM
            VM[i].allocated = VM[i].allocated + share
            extra_RAM = extra_RAM - share
        ENDIF
        // Allocate physically to the VM
        ALLOCATE RAM of size VM[i].allocated TO VM[i]
        PRINT "VM", i, " allocated ", VM[i].allocated, " GB"
    ENDFOR

    // 4. Ballooning/Dynamic adjustment (optional)
    // Monitor VM memory pressure and borrow unused memory via balloon driver.
END

Q4. Hypervisor Selection
Scenario Analysis:
We need to select between Type-1 (Bare-Metal) and Type-2 (Hosted) hypervisors.

Type-1 (e.g., VMware ESXi, KVM) runs directly on hardware, offers near-native performance, direct hardware access (VT-d/IOMMU),
but requires dedicated hardware management.

Type-2 (e.g., VirtualBox, VMware Workstation) runs on top of an existing OS, is cheaper/easier, but has higher latency and limited hardware passthrough.

Improved Pseudocode with Nuanced Justification:

BEGIN
    INPUT performance_required      // (HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW)
    INPUT budget_constraint         // (HIGH, LOW)
    INPUT hardware_access_needed    // (TRUE, FALSE) e.g., GPU/PCIe passthrough
    INPUT admin_skill_level         // (EXPERT, BEGINNER)

    // Decision Matrix
    IF performance_required == HIGH OR hardware_access_needed == TRUE THEN
        // Type-1 is mandatory for direct hardware access and low overhead
        SELECT "Type-1 Hypervisor (e.g., VMware ESXi, KVM, Xen)"
    ELSE IF budget_constraint == LOW AND admin_skill_level == BEGINNER THEN
        // Type-2 is cheaper (often free) and easier to set up on Windows/Linux
        SELECT "Type-2 Hypervisor (e.g., Oracle VirtualBox, VMware Workstation)"
    ELSE IF performance_required == MEDIUM AND hardware_access_needed == FALSE THEN
        // Either works, but Type-1 is still preferred for production stability
        SELECT "Type-1 Hypervisor (Recommended for production servers)"
    ELSE
        // Fallback
        SELECT "Type-2 Hypervisor for development/testing environments"
    ENDIF

    // Justification Output
    PRINT "Selected: ", selected_hypervisor
    PRINT "Reason: "
    IF selected_hypervisor CONTAINS "Type-1" THEN
        PRINT "- Provides direct hardware access, minimal latency, and robust resource isolation."
    ELSE
        PRINT "- Lower upfront cost, leverages host OS drivers, simpler deployment."
    ENDIF
END

Q5. Resource Limiting using cgroups (Control Groups)
Scenario Analysis:
To regulate CPU and memory usage per application, we use Linux cgroups (v2 recommended). This prevents a single rogue 
application from hogging system resources. We need to set absolute limits (e.g., memory.max) and CPU bandwidth limits (e.g., cpu.max) to ensure 
balanced performance.

Improved Pseudocode with cgroups v2 syntax:

BEGIN
    INPUT application_list          // List of (app_name, PID, cpu_limit, mem_limit)
    
    // Assuming cgroups v2 mounted at /sys/fs/cgroup/
    cgroup_root = "/sys/fs/cgroup/"

    FOR each app IN application_list DO
        // 1. Create a dedicated child cgroup for the application
        cgroup_path = cgroup_root + "app_" + app.name
        CALL mkdir(cgroup_path)
        
        // 2. Set CPU limits (e.g., 100000 = 1 core, 200000 = 2 cores)
        // Format: "max" or "quota period". Here, setting max bandwidth in microsecs.
        cpu_limit_file = cgroup_path + "/cpu.max"
        // If app.cpu_limit is 50%, set quota=50000 period=100000
        quota = app.cpu_limit * 100000 / 100   // Convert percentage to quota
        WRITE quota + " 100000" TO cpu_limit_file
        
        // 3. Set Memory limits (in bytes)
        mem_limit_file = cgroup_path + "/memory.max"
        WRITE app.mem_limit TO mem_limit_file   // e.g., "2147483648" for 2GB
        
        // 4. Optionally set Memory swap limit
        mem_swap_file = cgroup_path + "/memory.swap.max"
        WRITE app.mem_limit TO mem_swap_file    // Lock swap usage to same limit
        
        // 5. Assign the running process(es) to this cgroup
        procs_file = cgroup_path + "/cgroup.procs"
        WRITE app.PID TO procs_file
        
        // 6. Set controller enablement (for cgroups v2)
        subtree_control = cgroup_root + "cgroup.subtree_control"
        WRITE "+cpu +memory" TO subtree_control   // Ensure controllers are active

        PRINT "Applied limits for ", app.name, " (PID: ", app.PID, ")"
    ENDFOR

    // 7. Monitoring loop (optional)
    WHILE TRUE DO
        FOR each app IN application_list DO
            usage = READ cgroup_path + "/cpu.stat" and "/memory.current"
            IF usage > app.limit * 0.95 THEN   // 95% threshold
                LOG_WARNING(app.name, " approaching resource limit.")
            ENDIF
        ENDFOR
        SLEEP 5 seconds
    ENDWHILE
END


